    bio: "Mirza Muhammad Taraghay, known to history as Ulugh Beg — 'Great Ruler' — was born in 1394 in Sultaniyya, in what is now northwestern Iran, the grandson of the conqueror Timur the Great. He grew up in the glittering, terror-gilded court of the Timurid empire and was appointed governor of Samarkand at the age of sixteen. But while other princes spent their reigns on horseback, Ulugh Beg spent his in a different kind of pursuit: he wanted to map the sky with a precision the world had never seen.\n\nIn 1420, Ulugh Beg built an observatory outside Samarkand that was, by any measure, the greatest astronomical instrument of its age. Its centrepiece was a marble sextant of enormous radius — nearly forty metres — sunk into a trench cut into a hillside, aligned precisely with the meridian, and graduated so finely that a skilled observer could read off angles to within seconds of arc. With this instrument and a team of dedicated astronomers, Ulugh Beg spent decades compiling a new star catalogue: the Zij-i-Sultani, listing the positions of 1,018 stars measured entirely from fresh observation, without slavish dependence on Ptolemy or his predecessors. His value for the length of the sidereal year was accurate to within about one minute.\n\nUlugh Beg also established a madrasa in Samarkand that was famous across the Islamic world for teaching mathematics and astronomy alongside religious subjects — a combination that sat uneasily with some clerical authorities. He was a rare figure: a ruler who was also a genuinely original scientist, who computed trigonometric tables of extraordinary accuracy and wrote on observational method with a precision that any modern scientist would recognise. His observatory drew scholars from across the Islamic world, and his tables were eventually translated into Latin and used by European astronomers into the seventeenth century.\n\nHis end was violent and swift. In 1449, Ulugh Beg\'s own son, Abd al-Latif, launched a rebellion against him, motivated partly by political ambition and partly by the religious conservatism that his father\'s scientific enthusiasms had inflamed. Ulugh Beg was captured, sentenced to death on fabricated religious grounds, and beheaded. He was fifty-six years old. His observatory was destroyed by religious zealots within decades of his death. But his star catalogue survived — copied, translated, and carried westward — and the measurements he made on that hillside in Samarkand outlasted the empire, the dynasty, and the enemies who killed him."
